\section{Method}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:formulation}

Given model $\mathcal{M}$ and input $x$, we sample $N$ outputs $\{y_1, \ldots, y_N\}$ at temperature $T > 0$ via constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, where each $y_i$ is an entity set.
A \textbf{per-sample signal} $c_{\text{ps}}: (x, y_i) \to \mathbb{R}$ scores each output independently; an \textbf{instance-level signal} $c_{\text{il}}: (x, \{y_i\}_{i=1}^N) \to \mathbb{R}$ produces a single score per input (notation in Appendix~\ref{app:notation}).
This distinction is consequential for selection: in our study, 4 of 5 signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical QE scores to all $N$ candidates. Consensus-proximity (MBR) converts these to per-sample rankings (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}), but these rankings do not improve selection; only natively per-sample signals can, in principle, exploit oracle headroom.

\subsection{Confidence Signals}\label{sec:signals}

Throughout, entity matching uses exact span and type match (a predicted entity is correct iff its character offsets and type label both match a gold entity).
Four are \textit{consistency signals}: \textit{structural} (Soft Jaccard [SJ], entity voting confidence [VC]) and \textit{surface} (exact match [EM], Fleiss' $\kappa$ [FK]); log-probability [LP] is the sole \textit{model-internal}, per-sample signal.

\paragraph{Log-probability (model-internal, per-sample).}
For sample $y_i$ with tokens $t_1, \ldots, t_{|y_i|}$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:logprob}
c_{\text{lp}}(y_i) = \frac{1}{|y_i|} \sum_{j=1}^{|y_i|} \log p_{\mathcal{M}}(t_j \mid t_{<j}, x)
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Exact match voting (surface, instance-level).}
The proportion of samples producing the most common output:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:em}
c_{\text{em}}(x) = \frac{1}{N} \max_{y} \big|\{i : y_i = y\}\big|
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Fleiss' $\kappa$ (surface, instance-level).}
We adapt \citet{fleiss1971agreement} to entity sets: items are unique (text, type) tuples across all $N$ samples; each sample assigns binary present/absent labels, yielding a $|E_\cup| \times 2$ rating matrix:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fk}
\kappa = \frac{\bar{P} - \bar{P}_e}{1 - \bar{P}_e}
\end{equation}
where $\bar{P}$ is mean observed and $\bar{P}_e$ expected agreement.
FK relies on exact string matching (independence analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:fk_independence}).

\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (structural, instance-level).}
For samples $y_i, y_j$, we group extractions by type, compute max-weight bipartite matching (Hungarian algorithm, token-level IoU), and define:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sj}
\text{SJ}(y_i, y_j) = \frac{\sum_{m \in M} \text{sim}(a_m, b_m)}{\sum_{g} \max(|g_i|, |g_j|)}
\end{equation}
where $M$ is the matched set, $\text{sim}(a_m, b_m)$ is the token-level IoU, and $\max(|g_i|, |g_j|)$ penalizes length asymmetry.
The instance-level QE signal averages over all pairs: $c_{\text{sj}}(x) = \binom{N}{2}^{-1} \sum_{i<j} \text{SJ}(y_i, y_j)$.\footnote{When $E_\cup = \emptyset$, SJ is defined as 1 (perfect agreement on empty output).}
For selection, consensus-proximity converts instance-level signals to per-sample rankings via MBR utility (\S\ref{sec:mbr_theory}).

\paragraph{Entity voting confidence (structural, instance-level).}
For each entity $e$ in the union $E_\cup$ of all samples, we compute frequency $\text{freq}(e) = |\{i : e \in y_i\}| / N$.
The instance-level confidence is the mean frequency:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:voting}
c_{\text{vote}}(x) = \frac{1}{|E_\cup|} \sum_{e \in E_\cup} \text{freq}(e)
\end{equation}
When $E_\cup = \emptyset$, $c_{\mathrm{vc}}(x) = 0$.

\subsection{Evaluation Framework}\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Quality estimation.}
Spearman $\rho$ between the signal and instance-level F1 (per-instance entity-level F1 counting exact span-and-type matches), and AUROC classifying instances as above/below median F1.
Both quality estimation and selection exclude instances with no gold annotations: $n{=}529$ on SciERC, $n{=}2{,}756$ on CoNLL (Appendix~\ref{app:additional}).

\paragraph{Sample selection.}
Best-of-$N$ F1: per-sample signals (LP) directly rank candidates; instance-level signals rank via consensus-proximity (MBR utility; see SJ definition above).
Baselines: \textit{oracle} (highest-F1 sample) and \textit{greedy} ($T{=}0$ argmax decoding).
We define \textbf{instance degeneracy} as the fraction of instances where all $N$ samples yield identical F1;\footnote{``Identical F1'' means constant F1 across all $N$ samples for a given instance, not necessarily F1${=}1.0$. Samples may differ in surface form but produce the same entity set and thus the same score.} for these instances, no signal can improve selection.
We report F1 under the protocol matching each experiment's configuration (seed, epoch, $N$); each table specifies its protocol.

%% Prescriptive framework content is in Discussion §5 (sec:selection_method label there)
%% to stay within 8-page main body limit

\subsection{Entity Construction as MBR Decoding}\label{sec:mbr_theory}

Entity-level construction (\S\ref{sec:selection_method}) is a special case of minimum Bayes risk (MBR) decoding \cite{kumar2004minimum, eikema2020map}, extended from candidate selection ($\arg\max_{y \in \{y_1,\ldots,y_N\}}$) to construction over all subsets of $E_\cup{=}\bigcup_i y_i$.

\begin{proposition}[Entity construction as MBR for balanced set-overlap]\label{prop:construction_mbr}
Under entity-wise independence, the MBR-optimal constructed set with balanced set-overlap utility $u(\hat{y}, y') = |\hat{y} \cap y'| - |\hat{y} \setminus y'|$ is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mbr_construction}
\hat{y}_{\textup{MBR}} = \bigl\{e {\in} E_\cup : \hat{p}(e) > \tfrac{1}{2}\bigr\},\; \hat{p}(e) = \tfrac{1}{N} \textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{N} \mathbf{1}[e {\in} y_i]
\end{equation}
which is majority-vote entity construction (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_proof}).
\end{proposition}

\noindent\textbf{Remark.}
For typical NER outputs ($|y| \leq 30$ entities), balanced set-overlap and F1 differ by a multiplicative factor close to~1; we use them interchangeably in empirical sections.
F1-optimal thresholding requires solving for a global threshold \cite{dembczynski2011exact}; the constant $1/2$ threshold is a tight, computationally cheap approximation.

Signal-derived weights $w_i$ yield quality-weighted MBR: $\hat{p}_w(e) = \sum_{i} w_i \, \mathbf{1}[e {\in} y_i] / \sum_{i} w_i$, equivalent to self-normalized weighting with quality scores as unnormalized weights.
Weighted MBR tends to improve when $w_i$ correlates with $u(y_i, y^*)$ within instances; when this correlation is weak (LP on SciERC: $\rho{\approx}0.2$; few-shot: \S\ref{sec:scaling}), weighting degrades (\S\ref{sec:selection_method}).

\paragraph{Qualitative factors governing selection effectiveness.}
Three factors shape LP selection gains: (1)~\textit{instance degeneracy} (SciERC 17.8\%, CoNLL 61.7\%; Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}); (2)~\textit{oracle headroom} (CoNLL ${\approx}4$\,pp vs.\ SciERC 13.0\,pp); (3)~\textit{LP-quality alignment} (Few-NERD $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$ vs.\ SciERC $\rho{\approx}0.26$).
The pattern matches observations: Few-NERD (low degeneracy, strong alignment) yields $+1.39$\,pp; SciERC (weak alignment) $+0.04{\pm}0.48$\,pp (3-seed; Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}); CoNLL (high degeneracy) none; WNUT-17 shows reversed LP ($\rho{=}{-}0.446$) yet QE remains effective (VC $\rho{=}0.724$, $N{=}16$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}).
The factors do not combine into a predictive model ($R^2{<}0$, 23 configurations) but each is supported by controlled ablations (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}, \S\ref{sec:discussion}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}
\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered). Few-NERD/CoNLL: 3-seed means; SciERC: single-seed (3-seed in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). $p$: paired bootstrap.}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
WNUT-17 & 1{,}287 & .452 & 67.9\% & $-$.446 & $-$0.45\,pp & .72 \\
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 61.7\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .644 & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.0\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms LP selection at low degeneracy ($+1.39$~pp, $p{<}0.001$; structural signals degrade: VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp), with LP 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp), generalizing to LLaMA (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}). Training convergence modulates degeneracy (5-epoch: $31$\%, halving LP gains; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP direction.}
Across 23 configurations, degeneracy does not predict LP gain ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it causally modulates LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}). Cross-dataset prediction requires LP signal quality (Appendix~\ref{app:conditional_decomp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type analysis ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

Structural signals remain negative regardless of sample budget; $N$-scaling analyses are in Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robustness_main}

Consistency signals are robust across $T{\in}\{0.7, 1.0\}$ with sublinear $N$-scaling gains (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); signal rankings are stable across seeds ($W{=}1.0$; Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}). NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Scale ablation: 8B vs.\ 4B QE $\rho$ ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, seed 42). RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}.}
\label{tab:scale_ablation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}ll cc r@{}}
\toprule
& & \textbf{8B} & \textbf{4B} & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
CoNLL & SJ & .436 & .422 & $-$.014 \\
      & FK & .400 & .418 & $+$.018 \\
SciERC & SJ & .360 & .422 & $+$.062 \\
       & FK & .267 & .326 & $+$.059 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Scale robustness.}
With Qwen3-4B (Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation}), QE is comparable (mean $|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$), yet selection remains below greedy despite 13.0~pp headroom. Task difficulty is a stronger predictor than model scale within the 3B--8B range (Appendix~\ref{app:llama3b}).
Degeneracy scales with capability: 8B shows 17.8\% vs.\ 4B's 10.4\% on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:degeneracy}).

\paragraph{Controlled ablations.}
LoRA rank${=}8$ (vs.\ 32) lowers degeneracy to 7.2\% but LP remains below greedy ($\rho{\approx}0.24$), confirming low degeneracy and adequate alignment are independently necessary ($T{=}1.2$; \S\ref{sec:discussion}).
Supervised classifiers, a DeBERTa verifier, and hidden state probes all fail to close the gap (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}, \ref{app:hidden_probe}); the bottleneck extends to model-internal representations.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Scaling Behavior}
\label{sec:scaling}

We test whether the gap persists across scales and model families using Qwen2.5 (7B fine-tuned, 72B zero-shot and few-shot) alongside the primary Qwen3-8B.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-scale and cross-family comparison ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Hdr.\ = oracle $-$ greedy (pp). Degen.: instance degeneracy (\%). $^\star$Qwen2.5; unmarked: Qwen3. $^\dagger$Not computed.}
\label{tab:scale_comparison}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Regime & Greedy & Hdr. & Degen. & VC & SJ & FK & LP \\
\midrule
SciERC & 8B FT  & .644 & 13.0 & 17.8 & .379 & .360 & .267 & .205 \\
       & 72B ZS$^\star$ & .399 & 15.0 & 21.0 & .277 & .217 & .227 & .081 \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & 7B FT$^\star$ & .784 & 9.2 & 31.7 & .404 & .388 & .315 & .346 \\
         & 8B FT & .748 & 12.4 & 13.0 & .386 & .300 & .313 & .220 \\
         & 72B ZS$^\star$ & .628 & ---$^\dagger$ & 46.2 & .416 & .345 & ---$^\dagger$ & .119 \\
\midrule
CoNLL  & 8B FT  & .908 & ${\approx}$4 & 61.7 & .424 & .436 & .400 & .302 \\
       & 72B ZS$^\star$ & .712 & 5.3 & 67.7 & .297 & .304 & .302 & $-$.002 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

LP $\rho$ drops without fine-tuning (Table~\ref{tab:scale_comparison}) and LP selection fails on all datasets. Structural signals generalize (Few-NERD VC $\rho{=}0.416$). Degeneracy increases (Few-NERD: $46.2$\% vs.\ $13.0$\%; CoNLL: $67.7$\% vs.\ $61.7$\%); this comparison simultaneously varies model family, regime, and quantization, so individual effects cannot be disentangled.
LP-weighted construction yields $+1.53$\,pp on SciERC 72B ZS ($p{=}0.008$, CI [$+0.25$, $+2.82$]), recovering $10.2$\% of headroom. Construction fails on Few-NERD ($-0.20$\,pp) and CoNLL ($-0.37$\,pp) where degeneracy exceeds $46$\%---consistent with FT-regime degeneracy gating.

\paragraph{Few-shot demonstrations improve QE but not construction.}
Adding 3-shot demonstrations to 72B improves greedy on SciERC and CoNLL ($+3.3$--$6.2$\,pp) but not Few-NERD ($-0.9$\,pp), and strengthens structural QE (CoNLL SJ $\rho$ $.304{\to}.442$, LP $-.002{\to}.106$). Yet construction is ineffective across all three datasets (SciERC $-0.08$\,pp, CoNLL $+0.03$\,pp, Few-NERD $+0.02$\,pp; all n.s.). On SciERC, this failure despite low degeneracy shows the bottleneck is regime-dependent (the mechanism is not simple error correlation; Appendix~\ref{app:fs_error}); on Few-NERD, high FS degeneracy ($45.2$\%) places it in the expected failure regime.

\paragraph{Degeneracy is model-family-dependent, not scale-dependent.}
Qwen2.5 exhibits systematically higher degeneracy than Qwen3 on Few-NERD (7B--72B: $31.7$--$46.2$\% vs.\ Qwen3-8B: $13.0$\%; Table~\ref{tab:scale_comparison}), confirming degeneracy is shaped by architecture rather than parameter count. Construction fails for all Qwen2.5 configurations on Few-NERD (both n.s.); 3$\to$5 epochs triples degeneracy within the same model (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

Oracle F1 follows $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$ ($R^2 > 0.999$; Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_scaling}): FewNERD ($c{\approx}0.61$) scales comparably to reasoning tasks \cite{brown2024monkeys}, while SciERC converges more slowly ($c{\approx}0.35$); a single stochastic sample underperforms greedy ($N{\geq}4$ needed to match).
Despite growing ceilings, the gap limits recovery to ${<}1$\,pp.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}
\label{sec:prescriptive}

Since instance-level selection fails, we present two approaches that extract value from multi-sample generation without sample ranking.

\paragraph{Entity-level construction.}
Rather than selecting one complete output, we aggregate entities across samples, retaining those exceeding $\theta{=}2/N$, with LP-weighted or uniform scoring ($\theta$ stable in $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}). In zero-shot, uniform voting degrades due to noise entities; LP-weighting is necessary (SciERC $+1.5$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Entity construction vs.\ greedy ($\Delta$ macro F1; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, Qwen3-8B, multi-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). $\theta{=}2/N$. $p$: paired bootstrap vs.\ greedy. Best in \textbf{bold}. $^\dagger$Survives Holm--Bonferroni correction ($k{=}57$). $^\ddagger$LP-weighted not computed; uniform only.}
\label{tab:entity_construction}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Wt. & Greedy & Constr. & $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
SciERC & LP & $.646{\pm}.002$ & $\mathbf{.656{\pm}.003}$ & $+1.02{\pm}0.49$\,pp & .004 \\
       & Unif. & $.646{\pm}.002$ & $.653{\pm}.006$ & $+0.74{\pm}0.71$\,pp & .023 \\
\addlinespace
Few-NERD & LP & $.748$ & $\mathbf{.762{\pm}.005}$ & $+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp & ${<}.001^\dagger$ \\
         & Unif. & $.748$ & $.756{\pm}.006$ & $+0.74{\pm}0.58$\,pp & .003 \\
\addlinespace
CoNLL$^\ddagger$ & Unif. & .908 & .904 & $-0.39$\,pp & n.s. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

LP-weighted construction improves on Few-NERD ($+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; survives Holm--Bonferroni at $k{=}57$) and SciERC ($+1.02{\pm}0.49$\,pp, $p{=}0.004$, $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.20$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}), recovering 8--11\% of oracle headroom. CoNLL shows no gain ($-0.39$\,pp), consistent with high degeneracy. The mechanism is recall-driven ($N$-scaling in Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

\paragraph{LP-alignment gating (preliminary diagnostic).}
Construction and LP selection are complementary: construction dominates on SciERC ($+1.02$\,pp vs.\ LP n.s.; $\rho{\approx}0.2$), while Few-NERD favors both ($+1.40$ vs.\ $+1.39$\,pp; $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$).
We estimate degeneracy-adjusted $\rho_{\text{adj}}$ on 50 held-out instances: Few-NERD $0.516$, SciERC $0.167$, CoNLL $0.065$, WNUT-17 ${\approx}0$.
A three-way protocol ($\rho_{\text{adj}} \geq 0.3$: LP selection; $<0.3$ with positive construction: construction; else: greedy) routes Few-NERD to LP ($+1.39$\,pp) and the rest to greedy, stable across $\tau{\in}[0.2, 0.4]$.

\paragraph{Selective prediction.}
Orthogonal to construction, QE signals support selective prediction: at 80\% coverage, filtering low-confidence instances yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\phantomsection\label{sec:selection_results}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient, each serving a distinct role.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed where models achieve ${\approx}$65\% F1.
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control where limited oracle headroom constrains selection gains.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 51\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary where model capability is insufficient.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (training details in Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others due to earlier convergence; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
We draw $N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}, with $N{=}16$ for scaling experiments (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}
\label{sec:qe_results}

